    "media:imagetext": "airport the air raid alarm sounded and he had to taice the DC-two off and\nfly it away. It was a false alarm as the Japs raided a field between\nChungking and Wahsien but Sharp had to fly for two hours before he could\ncome back and land.\nThat afternoon about five both Sharp and Leonard went\nto lancow and took f1fteen passengers each to Chengtu leaving Hanicow about\nnine p.n. Everything went well except that both pilots ron into snow and\nice going over the mountains near Chengtu and both of them did not make\nthe time we hed hoped.\nSharp who was leading got out of Honkow on his\nsecond trip just at day light but Leonard who was about twenty minutes\nbehind ran into an air raid over Hankon. He saw the planes but as no raid\nwarning hed been given him he thought they were Chinese until he sew the\nbombs bursting on the airport.\nthen turned around and ran back to Ichang-\nThe air raid alarm service in\nHankow had been discontinued and evacuated.\nAfter he got to Ichang I sent him word to return to Chungking. He was delayed\nin getting gas at Ichang as we keep no gas on the field there, so he did not\nget back to Chungking until after trelve noon.\nHe hed been flying almost\nconstantly since live ofclock the afternoon before and had flown to Hongkong\nand back the night before that. I sent him home and told him his flighte for\nthat night were cancelled. He protested he was siright and would be able to\nfly but Idid not listen to him.\nWhen the raid, which Leonard just missed came over Hankow that\nmorning, all of Leonard's passengers were at the field waiting for the plane.\nWhen the raid cane they scramed in all directions. Some hid in the drain\nditch around the rield, sone jumped into old bomb holes and some ducked into\na dug-out nearby. Mechanic Haynie who stopped to look atter the passengers\nwas the last men to make the dug-out and the Japs were machine-gunning him\nnot return. It took five hours to get him out and they had to back \u0026 car up\nto the entrance.\nThe weather got worse until it becane clearly impossible to fly\nover the mountains to Chengtu at night with any degree of safety at all, so\nI wired Hankow we would have only one flight that night. The plane would\narrive just after dark and leave in the morning just at dawn for Chungking•\nThe replies we got to that from Hankow were really frantic.\nI decided I\nhad better go down that night and explain our problems to the Minister.\nAlso I wanted to see Socony and I wanted to check up on the evacuation of our\nstaff.\nThe flight down was uneventful, the weather was bad and we flew\ncontact all the way. We had a bad icing condition with the temperature only\nthree degrees above freezing.\nIf we hed pulled up, as me would have to do\nat night, it might have been difficuit. We got to Ichang and out of the\nmountains just at dark and on into Honkow a little after eight.\nThe field\npresented an eerie but interesting spectacle. It was a particularly dari\nnight and only small lanterns and flash lights were allowed. The field had\nbeen heavily bonbed recently and none of the holes were being filled up.\nAll over the field were gangs of coolies digging and we were told they were\nmining the field. I located the nearest bomb hole and made a mental note of\nits bearing from the plane and I was all prepared to dive in it if necessary.",
